{
 "duration": 74.794,
 "beats": [
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "You step outside, and the rain smells like a hospital. What if it rained pure alcohol?",
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 5.318
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "Your hand turns freezing. Alcohol evaporates much faster than water, and it pulls the heat right out of your skin.",
   "start": 6.018,
   "end": 12.747
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "Neighbors run out laughing, holding up cups. Bad idea. This is pure ethanol, far stronger than any drink, and it can poison you fast.",
   "start": 13.447,
   "end": 22.427
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "Then the trees begin to change. Alcohol strips the waxy coat off every leaf and drags the water out. The green turns brown.",
   "start": 23.127,
   "end": 31.036
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "The river won't save you. Alcohol mixes right in, microbes feast on it, and they use up the oxygen. The fish suffocate.",
   "start": 31.736,
   "end": 39.515
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "But the real danger is in the air. Above thirteen degrees Celsius, alcohol gives off enough vapor to catch fire. One car engine. One spark.",
   "start": 40.215,
   "end": 49.84
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "And you may not even see it. Burning alcohol gives a faint blue flame, almost invisible in daylight. You feel the heat before you see the fire.",
   "start": 50.54,
   "end": 59.048
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "Here's the strange part. That fire makes water. The steam rises, cools, and could fall again as ordinary rain.",
   "start": 59.748,
   "end": 67.391
  },
  {
   "voice": "michael",
   "text": "So this rain wouldn't get the world drunk. It would set it on fire. What should it rain next? Comment below.",
   "start": 68.091,
   "end": 74.794
  }
 ],
